"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a pathological stage of pT2, negative surgical margins, and no invasion of the seminal vesicles. The surgical specimen showed a Gleason 4+3 (ISUP grade group 3). Biopsy results showed a progression from Gleason 0+0 to Gleason 3+4 over time. The absence of lymph nodes removed introduces uncertainty regarding nodal status, as no nodes were sampled, meaning pNx is not equivalent to pN0. The negative margins and absence of lymphovascular invasion are favorable prognostic factors. The preoperative PSA was high at 9.5. The primary driver for prognosis is the negative surgical margins and lack of extension. Countervailing factors include the high preoperative PSA and the uncertainty introduced by missing nodal sampling. Postoperative PSA and its trajectory are critical unknowns that would significantly revise the prognosis. Missing postoperative PSA data prevents a definitive assessment of recurrence risk. The fixed estimated horizon is 59.96 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 38.98–80.95 months; its coverage has not been validated."
